; A homogeneous table whose KEY domain is (or contains) a float compiles to a `BTreeMap<f64, _>`
; (or an `OrderedHashMap` bounded `K: Hash + Eq + Ord` under --preserve-encodings), and floats
; implement none of `Eq`/`Ord`/`Hash` — so the emitted crate fails `cargo build` with E0277 in
; every case. Malformed emission is a hard generator error here, so these must reject GRACEFULLY at
; generation, not generate `ok` and defect downstream. This fixture pins the DIRECT float-key family
; (the domain resolves to a concrete rust type before finalize):
;   - `float64`/`float32`/`float` => domain is directly `Primitive(F64/F32)`.
;   - `number` (prelude `int / float`) => a TypeChoice with an F64 variant (PreludeNumber).
;   - `time` (tag 1 over `number`) => a Wrapper around PreludeNumber (PreludeTime).
; Both the top-level table shape AND the nested/inline shape must reject: a top-level `x = { k => v
; }` rule is a Table rust struct (its key is the `domain`, walked directly), whereas an inline
; `{ k => v }` used as an array element or map value surfaces as a `Map` conceptual type inside the
; enclosing struct's fields — two distinct emission paths, both covered here (`e`, `f`).
; The composite generic-instance case (resolved only during finalize) is pinned separately in
; float_table_key_composite.cddl. Remedy for a real consumer: an integer/text/bytes key domain, or
; an ordered-float wrapper if one is ever justified (see cddl-matrix/ROADMAP.md § findings).
a = { float64 => uint }
b = { float32 => uint }
c = { number => uint }
d = { time => uint }
e = [x: uint, { number => uint }, y: tstr]
f = { k: { time => uint } }
